  "worked_solution": "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-36x+16y & =-16 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}+4(0)^{2}-36x+16(0) & =-16 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-36x & =-16 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =-\\frac{16}{9} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-{2}^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{9} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{9}+4 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{9}+\\frac{36}{9} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =\\frac{20}{9} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(2-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)$ and $(2+\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
